  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    Wishai qingnu'Crest had done the math three times. The numbers were correct each time, which was the problem.
    
    Per Amendment Thirty-Nine of the Long-dark Edict, fire-core allocation during supply interruption followed rationing tiers. Tier One: nurseries and medical warrens. Tier Two: trade-essential infrastructure. Tier Three: residential terraces, distributed by population density. Wishai had counted the populations herself, walking each terrace with a tally-stone and a clerk named Iyoa who sneezed whenever they passed through corruption-touched ice. The upper terraces of Promenade Hei held four hundred and twelve residents. The lower terraces held ninety-one.
    
    The lower terraces held ninety-one because the corruption had crept through the crystal spires there, manifesting as cold-burn discoloration and a whispering that residents described, with careful vagueness, as *conversational*. People had moved upward. This was sensible. This meant the lower terraces, per the rationing formula, received fewer fire-cores. This was also sensible. This meant the ice down there grew colder, which meant the corruption spread faster, which meant more residents moved upward, which meant fewer fire-cores, which meant—
    
    Wishai filed Amendment Forty-Two. It adjusted the population-density formula to account for projected migration between terraces. It was precise. It referenced three prior amendments. It was carved into the Long-dark Edict's margin with a chisel so fine it could split a snowflake, because the center of the stone had been full since Amendment Twenty-Six—forty-seven layers of text carved over text, each disaster commemorated with bureaucratic precision. Thanksgiving. Schism. Aurora-touched. Darkening. Collapse. Gilding. Thaw. The ice remembers every one.
    
    The Nightshelf courier arrived that afternoon with eleven fire-cores instead of the requisite fourteen. Wishai asked why. The courier, a young penguin whose trade-name was Splitpath and whose flipper-ink glowed a steady amber, said the Nightshelf had read Amendment Thirty-Nine's supply clause as applying to the pre-Darkening baseline, not the post-Darkening adjusted baseline referenced in Amendment Forty-One. They were shipping per the amendment. Just not the same amendment.
    
    Wishai stood in the receiving hall of the Aerie Perch—the first structure the Qingqe'frostlings had raised, still standing, still functioning, still called The Watching by locals who did not explain why—and redistributed eleven fire-cores across a rationing system designed for fourteen. The lower terraces received one. It would keep a single brazier lit for perhaps nine days.
    
    She filed the distribution report. The colony's designation remained *thriving*, because the form to change it required signatures from three council members, and one had walked into the ice during the Darkening and hadn't come back, and no one had filed the paperwork to replace him.
    
    Below the berg's northern shelf, something that was not sound pressed against the ice. Grandmother Deep floated at the edge of the colony's light-scatter, her massive body barely moving, her left eye tracking the spires and her milk-white right eye tracking something else entirely. The Dreaming Tooth lay somewhere in the deep behind her—taken during the chaos of the Abyss Collapse Tide when the ocean floor buckled and the Wake-Singers lost three shamans and their attention simultaneously. She had not stolen it so much as collected it, the way one collects an interesting shell. It hummed against the current. The penguins' spires hummed back, catching frequencies their crystals were never cut for, and Grandmother Deep watched with the genuine, patient interest of someone observing an experiment she did not design but found instructive.
    
    The corruption climbed another three inches up the eastern spire that night. Iyoa sneezed.
    
    ---
    
    Depth-Seer Mingnaluchong xuei'Ice had been appointed to address the corruption problem, and he addressed it with the thoroughness the Qingqe'frostlings were known for: he wrote Amendment Forty-Three.
    
    Amendment Forty-Three established cleansing protocols. Teams of aurora-adepts would rotate through the lower terraces on a fourteen-day cycle, performing sanctification rituals at each corruption node. The protocols were detailed. The rotation schedules were elegant. The resource requirements were listed in precise columns: aurora-crystals per ritual, adepts per team, rest days per rotation.
    
    The protocols required aurora-crystals. Aurora-crystals could only be recharged under clear skies with active aurora. The Darkening had depleted the stockpile seventeen days ago. The skies had been intermittent since. The stockpile existed on paper—Amendment Thirty-Seven authorized the count—but the count had not been reconciled with Amendment Thirty-Eight, which authorized emergency disbursements during the Darkening, disbursements that had already occurred, from the stockpile that was now being counted.
    
    Neu fengfa'Shrine caught the contradiction first. She brought it to Mingnaluchong with the clipped Stack formality that barely concealed something harder underneath—the Qingqe'frostlings efficiency that meant handling things fast, hard, and without asking permission.
    
    "Emergency protocols," she said in the administrative hollow. "The Long-dark Edict grants authority during celestial disruption. Amendment Six specifies—"
    
    "Amendment Six addresses structural integrity during aurora-loss." Mingnaluchong's voice was careful, measured. Huddle-core calm. "Not cleansing operations."
    
    "Amendment Six addresses *survival*." Neu's flippers were steady but her eyes tracked the dimming crystals overhead. "The stockpile's already spent. We're authorizing rituals with crystals that don't exist against corruption that does. We need the Nightshelf to send charged reserves—"
    
    "Through proper channels. The Edict specifies consultation before emergency requisition."
    
    "The Edict has forty-three amendments and half of them contradict the other half." Her formality cracked just enough to show what was beneath—what the Qingqe'frostlings had done during the Orca Incursion, what they wouldn't let happen again. "We don't have time for—"
    
    "Then we amend."
    
    Neu stared at him. "You're going to *amend* during the crisis?"
    
    "I'm going to formalize the response. So when this ends—"
    
    "*When?*"
    
    "—we'll have clear precedent."
    
    He filed Amendment Fifty-One eight months later. It acknowledged, in language so careful it bruised, that the cleansing protocols established in Amendment Forty-Three were not achieving projected outcomes. The corruption had reached the mid-terraces. Fourteen residents had become Touched—functional but changed, speaking to presences others could not perceive, their eyes carrying that particular distance that made their neighbors check the ice behind them.
    
    The Touched still worked. They worked well, in fact—the whispers seemed to give them a focus that bordered on serene. They hauled crates and mended nets and stood watches at the perimeter where the aurora-light was thinnest, where no one else wanted to stand. They were, by any measure, productive members of the colony.
    
    Mingnaluchong filed Amendment Fifty-Six. It reclassified Touched residents as a protected labor category. It guaranteed their rations. It assigned them to the lower terraces, where the corruption was worst and the work was hardest and no one else would go. This was compassionate. This was practical. This meant the lower terraces now had a stable workforce. This meant the rationing formula, per Wishai's Amendment Forty-Two, allocated fire-cores to the lower terraces based on their increased population. This meant fewer fire-cores for the upper terraces. This meant conditions in the upper terraces deteriorated. This meant more residents became Touched.
    
    Mingnaluchong stood in the Aerie Perch and looked at the Long-dark Edict. The original text—Gilt-Hand Yeaiu qi'Gleam's merchant charter, the dream of exclusive aurora-crystal rights authenticated by the Directrix Mirror—was invisible beneath layers of amendment. Each layer a scar. Each scar a disaster. Each disaster caused by the scar beneath it. Deep in the carved layers, the colony's accumulated panic was laughing at him.
    
    He picked up his chisel. Amendment Fifty-Seven would address the labor-category issue. He had done the math. The numbers were correct.
    
    Below, in the dark water, Grandmother Deep rolled slowly, her scarred flank catching the spires' sick light. The markings along her body spelled something in the old tongue, but the penguins' generation had stopped learning those symbols when the dead god quit answering prayers. She remembered when penguins were food. Small, oily, quick. She had eaten dozens in her youth, before the silence made her curious about other forms of consumption. What they had built fascinated her—the towers, the lights, the documents. Especially the documents. She had watched Promenade Hei for three seasons now, tracking its slow transformation with both eyes, and she had begun to understand the system. Not its content. Its *shape*. Each amendment was a prayer to a god that answered only in the language of the next disaster. The penguins were writing scripture and calling it governance.
    
    The Dreaming Tooth hummed in the deep behind her. It had been marked by the Abyss Collapse Tidenew grooves in the old bone, patterns that caught light in ways that shouldn't be possible. It hummed at frequencies the penguins' spires answered, and the spires' corruption spread along those resonance lines, and no one in Promenade Hei had filed a form for it because there was no form for something that existed outside the system of forms.
    
    Grandmother Deep found this the most interesting part.
    
    ---
    
    Ningeihua Of arrived at Promenade Hei on a clear day with twelve fire-cores, a revised trade schedule from the Nightshelf, and a question about Amendment Forty-Two's population-density formula as it applied to Tier Three allocations under the Flipper Accord's supply-guarantee clause.
    
    She was a junior trade-auditor. She was competent. She had read the Long-dark Edict in full—all fifty-seven amendments, though the text beneath Amendment Twenty-Six was illegible and the margins after Forty-Three required a magnifying lens carved from aurora-crystal, which itself had been requisitioned under Amendment Thirty-Seven and not returned per the disbursement schedule in Amendment Thirty-Eight.
    
    Her question was simple: the Nightshelf's revised trade schedule referenced the pre-Darkening baseline. The Flipper Accord's supply-guarantee referenced the post-Darkening adjusted baseline. Amendment Forty-One bridged these two baselines but had been partially overwritten by Amendment Forty-Four, which established emergency procurement authority during corruption events, which were now continuous, which meant the emergency authority was now permanent, which meant the baseline was now—
    
    She stood in the Aerie Perch. The spires outside caught the aurora wrong—that thickness in the light that makes your eyes water, not quite shadow, not quite color. Iyoa sneezed somewhere in the corridor behind her. A Touched laborer walked past carrying a crate of depleted aurora-crystals to a recharging station that hadn't functioned since the Darkening. His eyes held that serene distance. He followed procedure.
    
    Ningeihua picked up a chisel. The ice waited—fifty-seven layers deep, the colony's accumulated panic preparing to be amended again. She was young. Ambitious. She'd studied every precedent, learned the careful dance of Stack formality that keeps everything from collapsing. She had the confidence of someone who believes the system makes sense if you're just smart enough to understand it.
    
    Per the amendment, she followed procedure. The designation remained *thriving*.
    
    ---
    
    In the water below Promenade Hei's northern shelf, Grandmother Deep watched the new clerk's shadow move behind the spire-glass. Her milk-white eye tracked the corruption climbing the eastern tower; her black eye tracked the penguin with the chisel. "Interesting," she said to no one—her voice blunt-object trauma, nothing penguin-elegant about it. "Prey creating rules for how to starve."
    
    The Dreaming Tooth hummed. The spires hummed back. Somewhere in the lower terraces, a penguin who had been Touched six weeks ago was explaining to a penguin who had been Touched six days ago where the tools were kept. Their voices were calm. Professional. They followed procedure.
    
    Grandmother Deep sank a few inches deeper, letting the cold water close over her scarred dorsal fin. She was patient. The kind of patience that outlasts amendments. She'd like to understand the system fully before it produced what it was producing.
    
    The chisel rang against stone. Amendment Fifty-Eight. A small correction to the rationing formula. Reasonable. Precise.
    
    Above the colony, the aurora pulsed—thin, uncertain, withdrawing like a breath held too long. Below, something vast shifted in the pressure-dark, drawn by frequencies that no amendment could address.
    
    A courier crested the ice-ridge to the south, sealed crate on her back, flipper-ink glowing amber. She walked past the seventeen skeletons at Midway Shelf without looking down. She had fire-cores for Promenade Hei. Nine of them. The amendment required twelve.
    
    She would arrive by evening. Someone would do the math. The numbers would be correct.
    
    The pattern holds.
    
    Barely.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771577186359_0", anchor: "He filed Amendment Fifty-One eight months later", text: "The chronicle is titled for Amendment Forty-Eight. The text describes Forty-Two, Forty-Three, and Forty-Four, then leaps to Fifty-One. The titular amendment falls in the gap — eight months of crisis compressed into a paragraph break. I have looked for it in the administrative ice. Amendments Forty-Five through Forty-Seven survive in fragmentary form. Forty-Eight does not. The chronicler named their work after the amendment no record preserves. Whether this is craft or confession I leave to the reader, but I am delighted either way.", type: :pedantic, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577186359_1", anchor: "what the Qingqe'frostlings had done during the Orca Incursion", text: "This is a Great Thaw chronicle. The Orca Incursion has not occurred. Neu's grandchildren's grandchildren have not been hatched. This is not ice-contamination or veil-bleed — it is a chronicler writing backward from their own era and forgetting to check the dates. The urgency Neu expresses is well-documented in Thaw-era sources; it derives from the Darkening itself, not from an event several ages hence. Chronicler error, the kind that survives because the sentiment felt true even if the referent was wrong.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577186359_2", anchor: "one had walked into the ice during the Darkening and hadn't come back", text: "Buried in a subordinate clause about paperwork. The Walking — the compulsion that seizes the grieving during the Long Dark, leaving them frozen with peaceful expressions or vanished entirely — took a sitting council member, and the colony's response was not mourning but a signature shortage. The designation stayed *thriving* because the dead couldn't countersign. One admires the priorities.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771577186359_3", anchor: "speaking to presences others could not perceive", text: "During the Long Dark the boundary between the living world and what the ice remembers thins. The Touched are not merely corruption-afflicted — they are perceiving across a veil the rest of the colony has the good fortune to ignore. That Promenade Hei's response was to reclassify them as a protected labor category and assign them to the worst shifts is the most administratively efficient response to metaphysical crisis I have encountered in forty-nine annotation sessions.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771577186359_4", anchor: "the Flipper Accord's supply-guarantee clause", text: "The supply-guarantee appears in the second secret draft of the Accord and was nearly struck from the third. The version the Nightshelf courier cites and the version Ningeihua cites may not be the same document. Both sides are arguing over a promise neither believed when it was carved. I noted the Accord's palimpsest nature in my annotation on 'Two Halves of the Vastness.' It is gratifying to see the redundancies performing exactly as designed — which is to say, badly, but not fatally.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771577186359_5", anchor: "Each amendment was a prayer to a god that answered only in the language of the next disaster", text: "Grandmother Deep has independently reached the same structural analysis of penguin governance that took me forty-nine annotation sessions. I am choosing to find it funny rather than mortifying that an orca matriarch who remembers eating us arrived at my conclusions faster.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771577186359_6", anchor: "Iyoa sneezed", text: "Iyoa's sinuses have now detected corruption advance twice in this chronicle — twice more than the amendment system has managed. Station this clerk at every corruption node. The Foundation Depths could use a methodology this reliable.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
